Revision history for Linux-Event-Async

0.002  2026-09-05
    - First stable release following the 0.001_001 developer version.
    - Require Linux::Event 0.110 and use its public resource hierarchy and
      Linux::Event::Framer native-consumer extension boundary rather than
      historical implementation package names.
    - Add Stream ready(), returning a Linux::Event::Async::Future that resolves
      with the same Stream at application readiness. Outbound readiness covers
      resolution, connect, and TLS handshake/verification where applicable.
    - Preserve normal core on_ready behavior alongside ready(): on_ready runs
      first, multiple Future waiters may observe the same one-shot readiness
      transition, and cancelling one readiness wait does not close the Stream.
    - Add Stream drain(), returning a Future that waits for an active
      high-water backpressure period to cross the configured low watermark.
      It does not require pending_bytes to reach zero.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Listener with persistent pull-based accept().
      Acceptance pauses while no wait is armed and uses one level-triggered
      accept per armed wait so excess connections remain in the kernel backlog.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Dgram with Future-based ready()/drain() and one
      persistent Awaitable for repeated packet recv(). List context returns
      payload plus Linux::Event::Address; scalar context returns payload.
    - Make Async Datagram receive pull-based by fixing
      max_datagrams_per_tick => 1 and edge_triggered => 0, leaving additional
      packets in the kernel receive queue until the next recv().
    - Preserve Datagram atomic send semantics, queue limits, socket policy, and
      high/low-water output backpressure. Drain cancellation remains waiter-local.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Timer with one persistent wait() Awaitable over
      Linux::Event::Kernel::Timer. Recurring waits preserve fixed-rate/coalesced
      expiration counts without one Future or Awaitable allocation per tick.
    - Make Timer wait cancellation wait-local. Core Timer cancel remains terminal
      and fails a pending wait.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Process with Future-based wait() over the public
      pidfd Process lifecycle. wait() resolves with the same Process only after
      core has reaped the pid and drained final stdout/stderr pipe data.
    - Support multiple Process exit waiters and waiter-local cancellation.
      Cancelling wait() never sends a signal, kills the child, detaches the
      Process, or changes explicit application lifecycle policy.
    - Keep Process stdout/stderr on the established callback drain path and keep
      write_stdin/close_stdin as core operations in 0.002 rather than inventing
      an unbounded or batching-ambiguous pull pipe API.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Signal with one persistent wait() Awaitable over
      the shared signalfd subscription. List context returns signal number plus
      the aggregated signalfd record count; scalar context returns the number.
    - Bound idle Signal delivery to at most one pending entry per subscribed
      signal number, accumulating counts per number instead of building an
      unbounded notification queue. cancel_wait() is wait-local while core
      Signal cancel remains terminal.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Event with one persistent wait() Awaitable over
      eventfd notification. Idle eventfd deliveries accumulate in one scalar;
      the Event remains notification-only and application payloads stay in the
      caller's real IPC/data channel.
    - Preserve Event thread/fork signaling boundaries. Worker interpreter clones
      may signal through the core handle but do not own Async wait state.
      cancel_wait() is wait-local while core Event cancel remains terminal.
    - Synchronize the vendored ordered-byte consumer ABI v1 header with the
      released Linux::Event contract, including read-side terminal events and
      host retain/release lifetime operations.
    - Guard callback-capable consumer host resume and pause calls with the ABI
      retain/release contract so reentrant Stream close and destruction are
      safe.
    - Return one persistent native Awaitable view from Stream recv, avoiding one
      Future or Awaitable allocation per received message.
    - Keep receive arming in XS and use a bounded 64-message, approximately
      256 KiB prefetch ring. Consecutive receives can consume retained messages
      immediately while bounded pause and backpressure remain intact.
    - Follow the currently awaited Linux::Event Loop when an async sub composes
      sequential operations across different Loops, including through nested
      child async subs.
    - Make native consumer teardown safe during Perl global destruction.
    - Add regression coverage for Stream readiness/drain/receive, Listener
      acceptance, Datagram receive/drain, Timer waits, Process completion and
      final pipe drain, Signal delivery/coalescing/cancellation, Event delivery
      and cancellation, multi-task composition, global destruction, and stress
      lifecycles.
    - Run release CI against Linux::Event 0.110 on Perl 5.36 and 5.44 with
      make test and make disttest, plus a current-core integration lane.
      Same-repository PR events skip duplicate jobs while fork PRs retain CI.
    - Declare the Test2::V0 dependency required by the Future::AsyncAwait
      alternative-Awaitable conformance suite.
    - Add explicit distribution provides/no_index metadata, repository resources,
      a Linux-only configure guard, and the Perl 5 license.
    - Rewrite public POD, README, and roadmap for the released architecture,
      including subclass framing/TLS policy, Stream/Datagram/Process tuning,
      readiness, backpressure, bounded pull/coalescing behavior, cancellation,
      and first-release scope.

0.001_001  2026-08-30
    - Initial development skeleton for the separate Linux::Event::Async
      distribution.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async import wrapper for Future::AsyncAwait.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Stream reusable receive Awaitable using the
      Linux::Event native Stream consumer ABI.
    - Add Linux::Event::Async::Future for async-sub result Futures.
    - Vendor the Linux::Event consumer ABI v1 header.
    - Return one persistent native Awaitable view from Stream recv, removing
      Stream hash lookup from the hot AWAIT_* methods.
    - Move Stream recv into XS so receive arming and Awaitable return no longer
      cross a Perl method trampoline.
    - Deduplicate repeated cancellation chaining to the persistent Stream
      Awaitable, keeping cancellation retention bounded across receive loops.
    - Batch native delivery behind the consumer ABI end-of-drain hook and a
      bounded 64-message, 256 KiB prefetch ring. Consecutive receives consume
      retained messages immediately instead of suspending through FAA once per
      frame, while consumer pause continues to provide bounded backpressure.
    - Add opt-in receive-path counters and separate receiver/producer CPU
      affinity controls to the matched native Stream benchmark.
